title: Request rate limit per customer key (from the key's policy)
updated: 2026-11-12T05:54:10Z
---

Generic capability: limit how many requests a single customer key may make, so an automated caller that runs away (a loop, a misbehaving agent) is stopped at the proxy before it burns capacity or budget.

Proposal
- Customer keys already carry a `policy` (customer_keys relation: key_id, name, policy, models). Add an optional rate block there, e.g. `{ rate: { per_s: 2, burst: 10, per_min: 60 } }`. Absent = no limit. A config-wide default may apply to keys without their own block; a key's own block may only be stricter than the default if the operator sets `max` (operator ceiling), never looser.
- Token bucket per key_id, in memory per Skeid instance (documented as per-instance; a shared store is out of scope for now).
- Over the limit: answer 429 with `Retry-After` (seconds) and an OpenAI-compatible error body (`type: rate_limit_exceeded`); do not forward upstream. Count the refusal in the usage store (status 429, a stable error_type such as `key_rate_limited`) without request content.
- Expose the current refusals per key as a Prometheus counter (key id hashed or omitted, operator choice) for alerting.
- Hot reload with the customer source: a changed policy takes effect on the next refresh; buckets of removed keys are dropped.

Acceptance
- Key with per_s 1/burst 1: second request within a second gets 429 + Retry-After, third after the wait passes.
- Key without a rate block: unlimited as today.
- Refusals appear in the usage store with status 429 and no content; nothing reaches the upstream node.

